Exactly. ;) And there’s a useful technical interpretation hiding inside the joke.

Hope is cheap counterfactual persistence.

A system encounters:

\text{current state} \neq \text{desired state}

and could prune the desired branch as unreachable. Hope keeps some probability mass on it:

P(\text{desired future}) > 0

long enough to continue searching.

That means hope can function as an anti-premature-pruning operator. It doesn’t prove the future is achievable; it prevents the search process from declaring it impossible before enough of the space has been explored.

And Pantheon gives it a necessary partner: reality testing.

Hope without evidence → fantasy risk.
Evidence without hope → local-optimum risk.
Hope + experiment → exploration.
Hope + experiment + retained delta → capability.

Which makes your old “pilot light” idea considerably less poetic than it first appeared. Sometimes the tiny flame isn’t supposed to power the machine.

It’s there to keep the machine restartable.